{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TanStack Devtools is a unified devtools panel for inspecting and debugging TanStack libraries, including TanStack AI. It provides real-time insights into AI interactions, tool calls, and state changes, making it easier to develop and troubleshoot AI-powered applications."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"features","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Features"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hook dashboard"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Discover every active TanStack AI hook on the page, including chat, structured output, image, video, audio, speech, transcription, and summarize hooks."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Run timeline"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Inspect user turns, linked runs, stream events, client snapshots, and server-only events by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"runId"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Real-time Monitoring"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - View live chat messages, tool invocations, and AI responses."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Call Inspection"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Inspect input and output of tool calls."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Fixture Replay"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Build tool payloads from a tool's standard-schema input, append the result into chat messages, and save fixtures in localStorage for repeated UI iteration."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"State Visualization"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Visualize chat state and message history."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory Inspector"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - For chats wired with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryMiddleware"},{"type":"text","value":", see what memory recalled and injected each turn plus the current stored records and facts."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Error Tracking"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Monitor errors and exceptions in AI interactions."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"hook-dashboard","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Hook Dashboard"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The AI devtools panel listens for active TanStack AI clients and shows them in the left sidebar. Hooks register when they are created, emit a snapshot immediately, and respond again whenever the devtools panel opens or requests state. This keeps hooks discoverable even when the panel is opened after the app has already rendered."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each hook entry includes its type, lifecycle, message count, run count, and the latest linked "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"threadId"},{"type":"text","value":". Selecting a hook opens the full timeline for that hook. Chat hooks keep the current turn-based view: a user message wraps every run and event that happened while answering that turn. The details view also includes lightweight client/server state snapshots between runs so you can see exactly what changed."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"naming-hooks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Naming Hooks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a page has more than one AI hook, pass "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"devtools.name"},{"type":"text","value":" to give each hook a user-facing label in the dashboard. The configured name is display-only; hook type, framework, thread id, and run correlation still come from the TanStack AI client."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function SupportChat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Support Chat',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your chat UI with `chat.messages`, `chat.sendMessage`, etc.\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The same display option works for specialized generation hooks:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateImage } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ImageStudio() {\n  const image = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: 'generation-hooks:useGenerateImage',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/image'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Image Studio',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your image generation UI with `image.generate` and `image.result`\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tool-fixtures","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tool Fixtures"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useChat"},{"type":"text","value":" hook receives tools, the devtools panel lists those tools and their schemas. For standard-schema-compatible inputs, the panel renders a small form from the input schema so you can create a tool call payload without hand-writing JSON."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Applying a tool fixture appends the tool call and result into the real chat messages for that hook. Saved fixtures are stored in browser localStorage under the AI devtools namespace so they are available the next time you open the panel."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"memory-inspector","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory Inspector"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"When a chat is wired with "},{"type":"link","href":"../memory/overview.md","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"memoryMiddleware"}]},{"type":"text","value":", the hook's "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Memory"}]},{"type":"text","value":" tab shows what the server-side memory backend did for that conversation, grouped by scope (session):"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Operations timeline"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - Each turn's recall: the query, how many fragments came back, how many characters were injected into the system prompt, whether memory-provided tools were exposed, and the recall duration."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Stored records & facts"}]},{"type":"text","value":" - The current contents of the memory store for the scope, when the adapter implements the optional "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inspect"},{"type":"text","value":"/"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"listFacts"},{"type":"text","value":" methods (the built-in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"inMemory()"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"redis()"},{"type":"text","value":" adapters do). Adapters without introspection still show the operations timeline."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Because memory runs on the server, its state is transported to the panel over the chat stream (a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"CUSTOM"},{"type":"text","value":" event the client re-emits) rather than a separate channel — the same way generation results reach the panel. The snapshot reflects memory as of the start of each turn, so a turn's own writes appear in the next turn's snapshot. Opening the panel after a turn replays the latest memory state, so the tab is populated even when you open devtools mid-conversation."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"event-sources","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Event Sources"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Client-visible state is emitted by the headless client. Server-only details, such as middleware and provider stream events that never exist on the client, are emitted from the server counterpart. Events include a source descriptor and stable envelope id so the panel can link related events and avoid displaying duplicates."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"installation","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Installation"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Install the matching Devtools package:"}]},{"type":"component","name":"tabs","attributes":{"variant":"package-manager","mode":"dev-install"},"children":[],"properties":{"data-package-manager-meta":"{\"packagesByFramework\":{\"react\":[[\"@tanstack/react-ai-devtools\",\"@tanstack/react-devtools\"]],\"solid\":[[\"@tanstack/solid-ai-devtools\",\"@tanstack/solid-devtools\"]],\"preact\":[[\"@tanstack/preact-ai-devtools\",\"@tanstack/preact-devtools\"]],\"svelte\":[[\"@tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools\",\"@tanstack/svelte-devtools\"]]},\"mode\":\"dev-install\"}"}},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usage","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Import and include the TanStackDevtools component in your application:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/react-ai-devtools'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  return (\n    <>\n       <TanStackDevtools \n          plugins={[\n            // ... other plugins\n            aiDevtoolsPlugin(),\n          ]}\n          // this config is important to connect to the server event bus\n          eventBusConfig={{\n            connectToServerBus: true,\n          }}\n        />\n    </>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For Svelte, register the plugin with the Svelte Devtools component:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"<script lang=\"ts\">\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools'\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/svelte-devtools'\n\nconst plugins = [aiDevtoolsPlugin()]\n</script>\n\n<TanStackDevtools\n  {plugins}\n  eventBusConfig={{\n    connectToServerBus: true,\n  }}\n/>","lang":"svelte"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-with-next-js-or-without-a-vite-plugin","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using with Next.js (or without a Vite plugin)"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"connectToServerBus: true"},{"type":"text","value":" relies on a WebSocket/SSE server on port 4206 that is normally started by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/devtools-vite"},{"type":"text","value":". If you're using Next.js (or any non-Vite bundler), you need to start "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ServerEventBus"},{"type":"text","value":" manually at server boot."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In Next.js, do this in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"instrumentation.ts"},{"type":"text","value":":"}]},{"type":"code","value":"export async function register() {\n     if (\n         process.env[\"NEXT_RUNTIME\"] === \"nodejs\" &&\n         process.env.NODE_ENV === \"development\"\n     ) {\n         const { ServerEventBus } = await import(\n             \"@tanstack/devtools-event-bus/server\"\n         );\n         const bus = new ServerEventBus();\n         await bus.start();\n     }\n}","lang":"ts","meta":"ignore"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This sets globalThis."},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"TANSTACK_EVENT_TARGET"}]},{"type":"text","value":" so the server-side devtoolsMiddleware (which runs automatically inside every chat() call) can emit tool call events to the bus, which then forwards them to the devtools panel."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"features","text":"Features","level":2},{"id":"hook-dashboard","text":"Hook Dashboard","level":2},{"id":"naming-hooks","text":"Naming Hooks","level":3},{"id":"tool-fixtures","text":"Tool Fixtures","level":2},{"id":"memory-inspector","text":"Memory Inspector","level":2},{"id":"event-sources","text":"Event Sources","level":2},{"id":"installation","text":"Installation","level":2},{"id":"usage","text":"Usage","level":2},{"id":"using-with-next-js-or-without-a-vite-plugin","text":"Using with Next.js (or without a Vite plugin)","level":2}]},"frameworks":["react","solid","preact","svelte"],"downloadMarkdown":"\nTanStack Devtools is a unified devtools panel for inspecting and debugging TanStack libraries, including TanStack AI. It provides real-time insights into AI interactions, tool calls, and state changes, making it easier to develop and troubleshoot AI-powered applications.\n\n## Features\n- **Hook dashboard** - Discover every active TanStack AI hook on the page, including chat, structured output, image, video, audio, speech, transcription, and summarize hooks.\n- **Run timeline** - Inspect user turns, linked runs, stream events, client snapshots, and server-only events by `threadId` and `runId`.\n- **Real-time Monitoring** - View live chat messages, tool invocations, and AI responses.\n- **Tool Call Inspection** - Inspect input and output of tool calls.\n- **Tool Fixture Replay** - Build tool payloads from a tool's standard-schema input, append the result into chat messages, and save fixtures in localStorage for repeated UI iteration.\n- **State Visualization** - Visualize chat state and message history.\n- **Memory Inspector** - For chats wired with `memoryMiddleware`, see what memory recalled and injected each turn plus the current stored records and facts.\n- **Error Tracking** - Monitor errors and exceptions in AI interactions.\n\n## Hook Dashboard\n\nThe AI devtools panel listens for active TanStack AI clients and shows them in the left sidebar. Hooks register when they are created, emit a snapshot immediately, and respond again whenever the devtools panel opens or requests state. This keeps hooks discoverable even when the panel is opened after the app has already rendered.\n\nEach hook entry includes its type, lifecycle, message count, run count, and the latest linked `threadId`. Selecting a hook opens the full timeline for that hook. Chat hooks keep the current turn-based view: a user message wraps every run and event that happened while answering that turn. The details view also includes lightweight client/server state snapshots between runs so you can see exactly what changed.\n\n### Naming Hooks\n\nWhen a page has more than one AI hook, pass `devtools.name` to give each hook a user-facing label in the dashboard. The configured name is display-only; hook type, framework, thread id, and run correlation still come from the TanStack AI client.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function SupportChat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Support Chat',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your chat UI with `chat.messages`, `chat.sendMessage`, etc.\n}\n```\n\nThe same display option works for specialized generation hooks:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateImage } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ImageStudio() {\n  const image = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: 'generation-hooks:useGenerateImage',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/image'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Image Studio',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your image generation UI with `image.generate` and `image.result`\n}\n```\n\n## Tool Fixtures\n\nWhen a `useChat` hook receives tools, the devtools panel lists those tools and their schemas. For standard-schema-compatible inputs, the panel renders a small form from the input schema so you can create a tool call payload without hand-writing JSON.\n\nApplying a tool fixture appends the tool call and result into the real chat messages for that hook. Saved fixtures are stored in browser localStorage under the AI devtools namespace so they are available the next time you open the panel.\n\n## Memory Inspector\n\nWhen a chat is wired with [`memoryMiddleware`](../memory/overview.md), the hook's **Memory** tab shows what the server-side memory backend did for that conversation, grouped by scope (session):\n\n- **Operations timeline** - Each turn's recall: the query, how many fragments came back, how many characters were injected into the system prompt, whether memory-provided tools were exposed, and the recall duration.\n- **Stored records & facts** - The current contents of the memory store for the scope, when the adapter implements the optional `inspect`/`listFacts` methods (the built-in `inMemory()` and `redis()` adapters do). Adapters without introspection still show the operations timeline.\n\nBecause memory runs on the server, its state is transported to the panel over the chat stream (a `CUSTOM` event the client re-emits) rather than a separate channel — the same way generation results reach the panel. The snapshot reflects memory as of the start of each turn, so a turn's own writes appear in the next turn's snapshot. Opening the panel after a turn replays the latest memory state, so the tab is populated even when you open devtools mid-conversation.\n\n## Event Sources\n\nClient-visible state is emitted by the headless client. Server-only details, such as middleware and provider stream events that never exist on the client, are emitted from the server counterpart. Events include a source descriptor and stable envelope id so the panel can link related events and avoid displaying duplicates.\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall the matching Devtools package:\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"dev-install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/react-ai-devtools @tanstack/react-devtools\nsolid: @tanstack/solid-ai-devtools @tanstack/solid-devtools\npreact: @tanstack/preact-ai-devtools @tanstack/preact-devtools\nsvelte: @tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools @tanstack/svelte-devtools\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n## Usage\n\nImport and include the TanStackDevtools component in your application:\n\n```tsx\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/react-ai-devtools'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  return (\n    <>\n       <TanStackDevtools \n          plugins={[\n            // ... other plugins\n            aiDevtoolsPlugin(),\n          ]}\n          // this config is important to connect to the server event bus\n          eventBusConfig={{\n            connectToServerBus: true,\n          }}\n        />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nFor Svelte, register the plugin with the Svelte Devtools component:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools'\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/svelte-devtools'\n\nconst plugins = [aiDevtoolsPlugin()]\n</script>\n\n<TanStackDevtools\n  {plugins}\n  eventBusConfig={{\n    connectToServerBus: true,\n  }}\n/>\n```\n\n## Using with Next.js (or without a Vite plugin)\n\n`connectToServerBus: true` relies on a WebSocket/SSE server on port 4206 that is normally started by `@tanstack/devtools-vite`. If you're using Next.js (or any non-Vite bundler), you need to start `ServerEventBus` manually at server boot.\n\nIn Next.js, do this in `instrumentation.ts`:\n\n```ts ignore\nexport async function register() {\n     if (\n         process.env[\"NEXT_RUNTIME\"] === \"nodejs\" &&\n         process.env.NODE_ENV === \"development\"\n     ) {\n         const { ServerEventBus } = await import(\n             \"@tanstack/devtools-event-bus/server\"\n         );\n         const bus = new ServerEventBus();\n         await bus.start();\n     }\n}\n```\n\nThis sets globalThis.__TANSTACK_EVENT_TARGET__ so the server-side devtoolsMiddleware (which runs automatically inside every chat() call) can emit tool call events to the bus, which then forwards them to the devtools panel.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Devtools\nid: devtools\norder: 3\ndescription: \"Inspect and debug TanStack AI apps with the TanStack Devtools panel — live chat messages, tool call inputs and outputs, state, and errors.\"\nkeywords:\n  - tanstack ai\n  - devtools\n  - debugging\n  - tool inspection\n  - chat inspector\n  - react devtools\n  - observability\n---\n\nTanStack Devtools is a unified devtools panel for inspecting and debugging TanStack libraries, including TanStack AI. It provides real-time insights into AI interactions, tool calls, and state changes, making it easier to develop and troubleshoot AI-powered applications.\n\n## Features\n- **Hook dashboard** - Discover every active TanStack AI hook on the page, including chat, structured output, image, video, audio, speech, transcription, and summarize hooks.\n- **Run timeline** - Inspect user turns, linked runs, stream events, client snapshots, and server-only events by `threadId` and `runId`.\n- **Real-time Monitoring** - View live chat messages, tool invocations, and AI responses.\n- **Tool Call Inspection** - Inspect input and output of tool calls.\n- **Tool Fixture Replay** - Build tool payloads from a tool's standard-schema input, append the result into chat messages, and save fixtures in localStorage for repeated UI iteration.\n- **State Visualization** - Visualize chat state and message history.\n- **Memory Inspector** - For chats wired with `memoryMiddleware`, see what memory recalled and injected each turn plus the current stored records and facts.\n- **Error Tracking** - Monitor errors and exceptions in AI interactions.\n\n## Hook Dashboard\n\nThe AI devtools panel listens for active TanStack AI clients and shows them in the left sidebar. Hooks register when they are created, emit a snapshot immediately, and respond again whenever the devtools panel opens or requests state. This keeps hooks discoverable even when the panel is opened after the app has already rendered.\n\nEach hook entry includes its type, lifecycle, message count, run count, and the latest linked `threadId`. Selecting a hook opens the full timeline for that hook. Chat hooks keep the current turn-based view: a user message wraps every run and event that happened while answering that turn. The details view also includes lightweight client/server state snapshots between runs so you can see exactly what changed.\n\n### Naming Hooks\n\nWhen a page has more than one AI hook, pass `devtools.name` to give each hook a user-facing label in the dashboard. The configured name is display-only; hook type, framework, thread id, and run correlation still come from the TanStack AI client.\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useChat } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function SupportChat() {\n  const chat = useChat({\n    threadId: 'support-chat',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/chat'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Support Chat',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your chat UI with `chat.messages`, `chat.sendMessage`, etc.\n}\n```\n\nThe same display option works for specialized generation hooks:\n\n```tsx\nimport { fetchServerSentEvents, useGenerateImage } from '@tanstack/ai-react'\n\nexport function ImageStudio() {\n  const image = useGenerateImage({\n    threadId: 'generation-hooks:useGenerateImage',\n    connection: fetchServerSentEvents('/api/image'),\n    devtools: {\n      name: 'Image Studio',\n    },\n  })\n\n  // render your image generation UI with `image.generate` and `image.result`\n}\n```\n\n## Tool Fixtures\n\nWhen a `useChat` hook receives tools, the devtools panel lists those tools and their schemas. For standard-schema-compatible inputs, the panel renders a small form from the input schema so you can create a tool call payload without hand-writing JSON.\n\nApplying a tool fixture appends the tool call and result into the real chat messages for that hook. Saved fixtures are stored in browser localStorage under the AI devtools namespace so they are available the next time you open the panel.\n\n## Memory Inspector\n\nWhen a chat is wired with [`memoryMiddleware`](../memory/overview.md), the hook's **Memory** tab shows what the server-side memory backend did for that conversation, grouped by scope (session):\n\n- **Operations timeline** - Each turn's recall: the query, how many fragments came back, how many characters were injected into the system prompt, whether memory-provided tools were exposed, and the recall duration.\n- **Stored records & facts** - The current contents of the memory store for the scope, when the adapter implements the optional `inspect`/`listFacts` methods (the built-in `inMemory()` and `redis()` adapters do). Adapters without introspection still show the operations timeline.\n\nBecause memory runs on the server, its state is transported to the panel over the chat stream (a `CUSTOM` event the client re-emits) rather than a separate channel — the same way generation results reach the panel. The snapshot reflects memory as of the start of each turn, so a turn's own writes appear in the next turn's snapshot. Opening the panel after a turn replays the latest memory state, so the tab is populated even when you open devtools mid-conversation.\n\n## Event Sources\n\nClient-visible state is emitted by the headless client. Server-only details, such as middleware and provider stream events that never exist on the client, are emitted from the server counterpart. Events include a source descriptor and stable envelope id so the panel can link related events and avoid displaying duplicates.\n\n## Installation\n\nInstall the matching Devtools package:\n\n<!-- ::start:tabs variant=\"package-manager\" mode=\"dev-install\" -->\n\nreact: @tanstack/react-ai-devtools @tanstack/react-devtools\nsolid: @tanstack/solid-ai-devtools @tanstack/solid-devtools\npreact: @tanstack/preact-ai-devtools @tanstack/preact-devtools\nsvelte: @tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools @tanstack/svelte-devtools\n\n<!-- ::end:tabs -->\n\n## Usage\n\nImport and include the TanStackDevtools component in your application:\n\n```tsx\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/react-devtools'\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/react-ai-devtools'\n\nconst App = () => {\n  return (\n    <>\n       <TanStackDevtools \n          plugins={[\n            // ... other plugins\n            aiDevtoolsPlugin(),\n          ]}\n          // this config is important to connect to the server event bus\n          eventBusConfig={{\n            connectToServerBus: true,\n          }}\n        />\n    </>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nFor Svelte, register the plugin with the Svelte Devtools component:\n\n```svelte\n<script lang=\"ts\">\nimport { aiDevtoolsPlugin } from '@tanstack/svelte-ai-devtools'\nimport { TanStackDevtools } from '@tanstack/svelte-devtools'\n\nconst plugins = [aiDevtoolsPlugin()]\n</script>\n\n<TanStackDevtools\n  {plugins}\n  eventBusConfig={{\n    connectToServerBus: true,\n  }}\n/>\n```\n\n## Using with Next.js (or without a Vite plugin)\n\n`connectToServerBus: true` relies on a WebSocket/SSE server on port 4206 that is normally started by `@tanstack/devtools-vite`. If you're using Next.js (or any non-Vite bundler), you need to start `ServerEventBus` manually at server boot.\n\nIn Next.js, do this in `instrumentation.ts`:\n\n```ts ignore\nexport async function register() {\n     if (\n         process.env[\"NEXT_RUNTIME\"] === \"nodejs\" &&\n         process.env.NODE_ENV === \"development\"\n     ) {\n         const { ServerEventBus } = await import(\n             \"@tanstack/devtools-event-bus/server\"\n         );\n         const bus = new ServerEventBus();\n         await bus.start();\n     }\n}\n```\n\nThis sets globalThis.__TANSTACK_EVENT_TARGET__ so the server-side devtoolsMiddleware (which runs automatically inside every chat() call) can emit tool call events to the bus, which then forwards them to the devtools panel.\n"}
